An unordered backlog; history lives in docs/plans/, docs/design/, and git.

The ranked survey behind the dbarts-capability items - what the seam is,
what each costs, and which need dbarts to grow first - is
docs/design/dbarts-capability-adoption.md. An item graduates to its own
docs/plans/<item>.md when it is picked up.

items
  callback-estimators: TABLED with its consumer (bartCause) 2026-07-16.
    The design and measurements are complete in
    docs/plans/callback-estimators.md (per-draw reducing callbacks
    store draws x estimators instead of n x draws surfaces; prototype
    +1.1 percent wall, bitwise ate; the stan4bart-side contract
    refinements - assembled response-scale ev to the callback, warmup
    gating, documenting the seam - are consumer-gated and land with
    adoption).
  dbarts-spec-adoption: adopt dbarts 1.0-0's exported dbartsSpec() in
    stan4bart_fit.R, replacing the hand-built dbartsModel and the
    dbarts:::parsePriors quoteInNamespace shim, and widen bart_args to
    the model-level knobs it makes reachable (family, monotone,
    interactions, blocks, variance, missing) rather than only names
    matching dbartsControl formals. Prerequisite for most of the items
    below; the shim already broke once at the 1.0 port.
  ordinal-outcomes: ordered-categorical responses via dbarts's
    cumulative probit. Structurally the existing binary path -
    getLatents into the parametric response, cutpoints drawn
    engine-side. Cheapest genuinely new family, and multilevel ordinal
    is a core audience need.
  log-lik-loo: surface dbarts_results.logLikelihood (per-observation,
    already computed for gaussian/binary/aft; the BART offset is the
    parametric mean, so what it reports is the joint conditional
    likelihood) as a log_lik extract type plus loo/waic methods.
    Storage opt-in, same discipline as store=.
  dbarts-passthroughs: wire, document, and test the features needing no
    new dbarts surface once dbarts-spec-adoption lands -
    storage="single", missing="incorporate" (MIA on BART-only
    covariates), monotone, sparse columns, warm starts, and
    interactions()/blocks(). The last is also the cheap R-side
    mitigation of the forest-ranef confounding dbarts investigated and
    declined to fix in the engine.
  per-observation-variance: robust-t residuals and a heteroscedastic
    variance forest, as ONE arc - both need per-observation precision
    to flow BART -> parametric, reversing today's scalar setSigma
    direction. Blocked on dbarts appending a working-weights/variance
    field to dbarts_results (additive, MINOR bump); this side then sets
    the parametric weights per iteration, which the Eigen model already
    carries but fixes at construction. dbarts-gated.
  aft-frailty: multilevel accelerated-failure-time survival.
    dbartsSpec(family = "aft", survival = ) already builds the
    specification; the open question is whether the imputed censored
    response is reachable through getLatents. Check that first - if it
    is, the rest is wiring, and multilevel survival is an unoccupied
    niche.
  multilevel-bcf: the two-forest (prognostic + treatment) BCF
    parameterization under random effects - the largest research payoff
    here, and what bartCause would consume. Needs a new dbarts.h entry
    point: BCF is built by its own path inside dbarts and is not
    exposed through the flat C API at all. decision-gated.
  vendored-header-refresh: DONE 2026-07-26. src/include/ and the rc
    sources are vendored from dbarts by copying the file and rewriting
    external/ -> ext/ (this package names that directory ext, dbarts
    names it external). rc_util.c, rc_bounds.c, rc/bounds.h, ext/R.h,
    ext/Rinternals.h, and ext/io.h all now match upstream, the ones
    with an external/ include modulo that line. Nothing left to sync.
    misc/ is down to stddef.h, which is deliberately NOT vendored:
    misc_index_t is a dbarts kernel type this package does not use.
    The misc/intrinsic.h question this entry used to hold open is
    closed by deletion, mirroring dbarts 091ffb9. The header was only
    ever reached through misc_adaptiveRadixTree.c, whose only caller
    parsed sampler control argument names, so the tree and the misc
    members it carried (string, memalign, intrinsic, alloca) are gone
    and the name matching is a direct scan in parametric_control.cpp.
    Nothing SIMD, aligned, or alloca-shaped is left in this package,
    and no translation unit includes config.h, so AX_EXT, the vendored
    m4 macros, AC_CONFIG_HEADERS, config.h.in/.win, and configure.win
    went with it. Do not re-vendor any of them: if a future sync pulls
    in a dbarts source that wants misc/intrinsic.h, it will also want
    upstream's AX_COMPILER_EXT configure side, and the two must move
    together - the macro names look cosmetic and are not
    (COMPILER_SUPPORTS_SSE2 asks whether the compiler accepts -msse2;
    the old HAVE_SSE2 asked whether the build host's cpuid reported
    it).
  bench-perf-rerecord: DONE 2026-07-27, bench-perf-3e2036b.csv is
    current. install 15.1s -> 11.8s, which is what the radix-tree
    deletion bought (9 translation units to 7). The negative
    per-iteration times that blocked the first attempt were NOT a
    quiet-machine problem - the machine was 83% idle by top's
    instantaneous sample, and load average and ps %CPU both misread it
    because they decay. The estimator was under-resolved: its 100/300
    iter spread was sized for ~0.5 ms/iter and sampling is now ~0.13
    ms/iter, so the signal fell under subprocess jitter. Widened to
    200/2000 in 3e2036b, which reproduces to ~10%.
  walnuts-periter-4x: per-iteration cost dropped ~4x (0.5 -> 0.13 ms
    across all three reference fits) somewhere between 174b369
    (2026-07-15) and 3e2036b (2026-07-27), and nothing claims credit.
    Not the radix-tree deletion, which cannot touch per-iteration cost
    - the estimator differences two iter counts, so per-fit setup
    cancels.
    A REAL SPEEDUP IS THE LIKELIER EXPLANATION, not measurement error,
    even though the estimator was under-resolved by then. Size the
    error: today's narrow readings scatter over 45-155 us, so jitter
    is about +/-55 us/iter, which against the old 0.5 ms/iter regime
    is 10-20% - it cannot manufacture a 4x gap. The narrow spread was
    adequate at 0.5 ms/iter (a ~100 ms signal) and only broke once the
    cost fell far enough to leave ~20 ms.
    Corollary, and the reverse of what this entry said first: the
    12-39x sampler-swap payoff in bench-perf-2ee6048.csv is if
    anything UNDERSTATED. It divides Stan-era cb809b1 (15.5/8.1/13.5
    ms/iter) by WALNUTS 2ee6048, and at 15.5 ms/iter the Stan side is
    a 3.1 SECOND signal - thousands of times clear of the jitter that
    broke the WALNUTS side. Only the denominator was ever fragile, and
    shrinking a denominator grows the ratio: at today's resolved
    figures the same comparison is 66-125x. NEWS.md's 12-39x is
    therefore safe to ship as-is.
    To close: bisect 174b369..3e2036b with the widened 200/2000
    spread. It is a short range and the win, if real, belongs in
    NEWS.md.
  release: stan4bart 0.0-14, in lockstep with dbarts 1.0-0. dbarts
    ships first; its TODO owns the ordered procedure. LAST.
